<Item XName="PoorMansDPD" UserName="PoorMansDPD" Type="Equation">
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>%this DPD model negates the normalized AMAM and AMPM data&#x000D;&#x000A;% to use it as a correction factor to return to linear Pin-Pout&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;%InstantaneousPowerDBM=30+10*log10((abs(input)).^2/(2*Rref));&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;LUTmin=min(powerdata);&#x000D;&#x000A;LUTmax=max(powerdata);&#x000D;&#x000A;LUTlen=length(powerdata);&#x000D;&#x000A;&#x000D;&#x000A;%small-signal gain values&#x000D;&#x000A;GainSS=gaindata_absolute(1);&#x000D;&#x000A;PhaseSS=phasedata_absolute(1);&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;% normalize the traces, if necessary, from large-signal S21 data&#x000D;&#x000A;GainCompress=gaindata_absolute-gaindata_absolute(1);&#x000D;&#x000A;PhaseCompress=phasedata_absolute-phasedata_absolute(1);&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;% If you do Gain Expansion, then you are moving farther up into the &#x000D;&#x000A;% amplifier compression.  Look ahead to that level, and make a &#x000D;&#x000A;% first-order correction with even more expansion.  This simple &#x000D;&#x000A;% technique gets you about 90% of the way, and 10-15dB of ACPR &#x000D;&#x000A;% improvement, assuming no memory effects.  (It relies on nice slopes)&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;PowerExpand=powerdata-GainCompress;&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;% Now control extrapolation manually, by limiting to within the range of LUT&#x000D;&#x000A;for ix=1:LUTlen&#x000D;&#x000A;    if (PowerExpand(ix) &gt; LUTmax)&#x000D;&#x000A;        PowerExpand(ix)=LUTmax;&#x000D;&#x000A;    end&#x000D;&#x000A;    if (PowerExpand(ix) &lt; LUTmin)&#x000D;&#x000A;        PowerExpand(ix)=LUTmin;&#x000D;&#x000A;    end&#x000D;&#x000A;end &#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;% invert the GCOMP data into an expander, with the power correction&#x000D;&#x000A;gainDB=-interp1(powerdata,GainCompress,PowerExpand);&#x000D;&#x000A;phaseDEG=-interp1(powerdata,PhaseCompress,PowerExpand);&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;% if you need, here&apos;s how to make a single complex value for a Math block.&#x000D;&#x000A;% the main downside to the math blocks is that they are 100x slower than C++.&#x000D;&#x000A;% gainpolar=10^(gainDB/20)*exp(i*phaseRAD);&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;% now format the matrix into data &quot;triplets&quot; for the compiled Amplifier model.&#x000D;&#x000A;xxx=reshape(gainDB,1,LUTlen);&#x000D;&#x000A;yyy=reshape(phaseDEG,1,LUTlen);&#x000D;&#x000A;zzz=reshape(powerdata,1,LUTlen);&#x000D;&#x000A;%&#x000D;&#x000A;myGCOMPtable=reshape([zzz;xxx;yyy],1,3*LUTlen);&#x000D;&#x000A;</Text>
